\description{
Gives every location in the study area to its nearest input point, so
each point gets one cell. Use it when the observations themselves define
the cells (sampling sites, monitoring stations, service points): cell size
then follows sampling density, with small cells where the points are dense
and large ones where they are sparse. Use \code{\link[=create_grid_polygons]{create_grid_polygons()}} instead
for equal-area cells, or for a resolution that does not depend on where
the data are.
}
\details{
\code{\link[sf:st_voronoi]{sf::st_voronoi()}} builds the diagram. This function projects lon/lat
input, builds and buffers an envelope so edge cells are bounded, clips to
\code{boundary}, restores the point-to-cell correspondence that \code{st_voronoi()}
scrambles, and numbers the cells with stable \code{cell_id} values.

The generators are the points' vertices, not the features. A MULTIPOINT
feature with several vertices therefore gets one cell per vertex, and its
\code{index} entry is the smallest \code{cell_id} among the cells it touches; the
others are referenced by no feature. Other functions in the package
(\code{\link[=prep_model_data]{prep_model_data()}}, \code{\link[=make_folds]{make_folds()}}) reduce such a feature to its
centroid instead, so cast to POINT, or take centroids, first if one cell
per feature is what you want.

With no \code{boundary} the cells are clipped to a boundary derived from the
data. For observations that is their own convex hull grown by 2 percent of
its diagonal, so every observation has a cell. Seeds are different: k-means
centres, random points in a study area and the centres a resolution
profile scored all lie inside the ground they stand for, and cells clipped
to the hull of the seeds leave the edge of that ground uncovered. The
boundary is therefore derived from the \code{cover} when
there is one: the argument, or the cover the seeding functions attach to
their result. A layer that looks like seeds and has neither a boundary nor
a cover (seeds bound with \code{rbind()}, which drops the attribute, or built
by hand) gets the seeds' own hull and a warning of class
\code{"spatialkit_no_cover"}.

A seed stands for its cell, so \code{index} gives each seed the cell it
generates. A k-means seed is a mean of observations and can lie outside a
\code{boundary} that is not convex, in a bay or a bend of a coast (see
\code{\link[=get_voronoi_seeds]{get_voronoi_seeds()}}). Its cell, the part of the study area nearer it
than any other seed, is built, and its \code{index} entry is that cell's
\code{cell_id}, although the seed lies in no cell. A seed is \code{NA} only when
every part of the study area is nearer another seed, so the clip removed
its cell whole, or when it has no location (its geometry is empty, or a
coordinate is not finite or lies beyond 1e150). The points are seeds when
a seeding function returned them (\code{\link[=get_voronoi_seeds]{get_voronoi_seeds()}},
\code{\link[=voronoi_seeds_kmeans]{voronoi_seeds_kmeans()}}, \code{\link[=voronoi_seeds_random]{voronoi_seeds_random()}}, \code{rbind()} of such
layers included), when they are the seeds of a \code{\link[=select_resolution]{select_resolution()}}
result, and when a \code{cover} is passed with them. A selection's seeds are
seeds by the attributes they carry, which \code{rbind()} and a write to a file
drop. Any other points are indexed by the cell that holds them, so a
point outside the study area is \code{NA}, unless it lies within a thousandth
of the median cell width of a cell, whose ID it then takes (see \code{index}
in \code{\link[=build_tessellation]{build_tessellation()}}); that includes observations that
carry a selection's record of the count, as
\code{\link[=assign_features_to_polygons]{assign_features_to_polygons()}} returns them. Seeds placed by hand, and
a selection's seeds that lost their attributes, are seeds once they pass
through \code{get_voronoi_seeds(method = "provided", seeds = )}.
}
\section{Seeds a profile scored}{

The seeds of a \code{\link[=select_resolution]{select_resolution()}} result, and the seeds
\code{\link[=get_voronoi_seeds]{get_voronoi_seeds()}} or \code{\link[=voronoi_seeds_kmeans]{voronoi_seeds_kmeans()}} return for one, are the
centres \code{\link[=resolution_profile]{resolution_profile()}} scored, and their record of the count
(\code{attr(, "count_rule")}) says so (\code{scored_cells = TRUE}) and holds the
centres in the CRS the profile worked in. (On a profile without an
estimate fitted to a subsample the seeds are those centres refitted to
every point, which carry \code{attr(, "refit")}; their record says
\code{scored_cells = FALSE}, and their cells are built as other seeds' are.
So are seeds fewer or more than the record's count, such as a
selection's seeds cut down to some of its rows: they are not the centres
the profile scored, and their record is handed on with
\code{scored_cells = FALSE}, as \code{\link[=get_voronoi_seeds]{get_voronoi_seeds()}} (\code{method = "provided"})
marks such seeds.)
Unless a projected \code{crs} is
given, their cells are built in that CRS, whatever CRS the seeds arrive
in (degrees, or the CRS of a boundary they were placed in), and returned
in it, or in a geographic \code{crs} when one is given. When the seeds are
those centres (as many, each at a different centre, within a thousandth
of its distance from the next nearest), each is first put at its centre
as the record holds it, so rounding the seeds took on in another CRS
does not move an edge. The cells are then the cells each row was scored
in, and \code{\link[=assign_features_to_polygons]{assign_features_to_polygons()}} gives a point on an edge between
two of them the cell the profile scored it in.
}

\section{Points far from the origin of their CRS}{

Points so far from the origin of their CRS that doubles there are spaced
more than a millionth of their extent apart (0.125 at 1e15, beside a layer
a few kilometres across) have the cells' edges and their own cells read
at that rounding, so two points that close can fall in one cell.  The
cells are built, and a warning of class \code{"spatialkit_xy_imprecise"}
gives the spacing and the extent.
}

\examples{
library(sf)
set.seed(1)
pts <- st_as_sf(
  data.frame(x = 5e5 + runif(15, 0, 100), y = 5e6 + runif(15, 0, 100)),
  coords = c("x", "y"), crs = 32632
)
res <- create_voronoi_polygons(pts, quiet = TRUE)
res$cells   # one polygon per unique point, with stable cell_id
res$index   # cell_id assignment for each input point
}
